/* ==============================================================
   02 — MoveJoints: the joint-space move
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     Meca500 only (R3 or R4)
   TEACHES   What a joint move is, and when to reach for it
   NEEDS     Robot activated + homed, no tool mounted
   WATCH     All six joints start together and stop together
   TIME      ~25 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   WHAT IS A JOINT MOVE?

   A Meca500 has six rotary joints. A joint move tells each one
   the ANGLE it should end up at. Nothing else.

       MoveJoints(t1, t2, t3, t4, t5, t6)

   Six numbers, in degrees, one per joint, numbered from the base
   (joint 1) to the flange (joint 6). All zeros is the robot's
   "zero position", the reference position marked on the robot
   body. Worth knowing what it actually looks like: the flange
   ends up pointing HORIZONTALLY FORWARD, not upward — roughly
   190 mm out from the base axis and 309 mm up from the bench.

   This is the most direct thing you can ask a robot to do. There
   is no maths between your command and the motors: you name the
   angles, the joints rotate to them.

   THE PART CUSTOMERS ASK ABOUT

   The robot does NOT move each joint one after another, and it
   does not move them all at full speed until each arrives. It
   SYNCHRONISES them: every joint starts at the same instant, and
   every joint arrives at the same instant. The joint with the
   furthest to travel sets the pace; the others are slowed to
   match. That is what makes the motion look like one gesture
   instead of six.

   THE TRADE-OFF

   Because you specified angles and not a position in space, the
   TOOL TIP takes whatever path falls out of the geometry. It is
   smooth and it is repeatable — run it a thousand times and you
   get the same curve — but it is an arc, not a straight line,
   and it is hard to predict by eye.

   So:
     - Use MoveJoints to REPOSITION.  Getting from one side of
       the cell to the other, going to a home or a park position,
       clearing an obstacle. It is the fastest and least
       constrained way to move, and it can never fail because of
       a singularity.
     - Use MoveLin when the PATH ITSELF matters. Pulling a part
       straight out of a fixture, laying a bead, entering a
       chuck. See 04_move_lin.mxprog.

   A joint move only fails for one reason: an angle outside the
   joint's limits. The robot checks this before it starts moving,
   so a bad target does not produce a half-completed motion — it
   produces an error and a robot that has not moved.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode — so this
   program starts from firmware defaults no matter what ran
   before it. Reactivating brings the motors back.

   This does NOT move the arm: a robot that was already homed
   needs no re-homing. (With an MEGP 25* gripper fitted, the
   gripper re-homes on reactivation. The robot still does not
   move — but keep the fingers clear.)

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision and calibration config all
   survive. Run common/cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
Home()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


// Slow, so the demo is legible and safe to stand next to.
// This is a percentage of maximum joint speed, not a velocity.
// Raise it to 100 to show the real cycle time.
SetJointVel(15)


// --- Start from the zero position -----------------------------
// All six joints at 0 deg, flange pointing forward.
// Every example in basic/ starts and ends here, so you can run
// them back to back without re-jogging the robot.
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)

Delay(1)


// --- One joint at a time --------------------------------------
// Only the first number changes, so only joint 1 turns: the whole
// arm swings 45 deg about the base. This is the clearest way to
// show someone what the six numbers actually mean.
MoveJoints(45, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)

Delay(1)

// Now joints 2 and 3 reshape the arm, while joint 1 stays where
// it is. Watch the tool tip: it swings through an arc.
// Nobody asked for that arc — it is simply what the geometry does
// when those two joints rotate.
MoveJoints(45, -30, 45, 0, 0, 0)

Delay(1)


// --- All six at once ------------------------------------------
// The interesting one. Every joint has a different distance to
// travel, and joint 1 has the furthest (from +45 to -45).
//
// Watch the start and the finish, not the middle: everything
// begins moving in the same instant and everything settles in
// the same instant. That is the synchronisation described above.
MoveJoints(-45, -30, 45, 0, 60, 90)

Delay(1)


// --- Back to zero ---------------------------------------------
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. Select any single MoveJoints line and press Ctrl+Enter to
      run just that line. Change one number, run it again. This
      is the fastest way to make the six-numbers idea land.

   2. Change SetJointVel(15) to SetJointVel(100) and run the whole
      file. Same path, same targets — dramatically shorter cycle.
      Speed is a setting, not a rewrite.

   3. Try MoveJoints(0, 0, 100, 0, 0, 0). The robot refuses and
      reports an error, because joint 3 stops at 70 deg. Note
      that it refuses BEFORE moving. Then press the run button
      (now red) to reset the error, and resume.

   NEXT   03_move_pose.mxprog — naming a position in space
          instead of a set of angles.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
